<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <Import Project="..\..\Build\OutWit.Controller.Scripts.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ControllerName>Corpus</ControllerName>
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
    <Description>Bundled .wit scripts for the OutWit Corpus controller — CorpusProcess (public-corpus shards fetched by the nodes, cleaned, language-tagged, quality-filtered and signed, with the tasks and the results travelling as blobs, sampled re-execution, a repair round for shards a node could not answer, on two machines when the run samples, a dispute round by three machines for copies that disagree, and that round once more for disputes that came back with too few copies), CorpusPreflight, CorpusDedup (the near-duplicate merge over a run) and CorpusRuntimeDiagnostics (the module's diagnostics and the capabilities document).</Description>
    <PackageTags>corpus;common-crawl;scripts;witengine;witcloud;controller</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Compatibility marker (see docs/adr-001-script-controller-version-compatibility.md).
       ENABLE WHEN THE CONTROLLER IS FIRST PUBLISHED TO NUGET.ORG, which is its 1.0.1 at the
       earliest (when a later version is the first one there, the lower bound moves to it
       before the marker is enabled, and the test's constant with it). With that, the range
       is the one to enable as it stands. Lower bound: the first controller version that feed
       will have (the decision record: a lower bound no feed has is noise at every restore). The
       scripts themselves need what a controller has done since 0.9.0: they ask Corpus.Disputes
       for a second dispute round (six arguments) and hand it to Corpus.Collect and
       Corpus.Integrity (nine arguments); Corpus.RepairRate and the run's options in
       Corpus.Integrity are from 0.7.0, packages and Corpus.ProcessPackage from 0.5.0. Upper
       bound: the next major, exclusive.
       NOT ENABLED YET, and it cannot be: the reference is restored from the feeds this
       repository restores from, and no controller version of the range is on nuget.org (the
       0.x and 1.0.0 controller packages went to the organization's GitHub Packages feed only).
       Enabled now it breaks the restore of this project, the publish job included. Enable it
       (remove the comment marks around the ItemGroup, nothing else) as the first step after the
       controller package of the release is on nuget.org, before this package is packed for it.
       A test holds the range to the versions of the build, and fails a build of a controller
       after 1.0.1 in which the marker is still off (CorpusScriptsCompatibilityTests).
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="OutWit.Controller.AI.Corpus" Version="[1.0.1, 2.0.0)" />
  </ItemGroup>
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\OutWit.Controller.AI.Corpus\Scripts\*.wit"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="content/scripts/" />

    <None Include="build\OutWit.Controller.AI.Corpus.Scripts.targets"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="build\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Import Project="..\..\Build\OutWit.Controller.Scripts.targets" />

</Project>
